  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 AM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nA short wave trough is currently situated over the Rockies and is \nexpected to pass over the Plains later today. Ahead of this feature, \ncloud cover overspread the state yesterday and into the overnight \nhours. High pressure continues to dominate at the surface and light \neasterly to northeasterly winds prevail. On satellite, we are seeing \nsome lower clouds across western Kansas along with a few light \nreturns showing up on radar. Some lower clouds may overspread our \ncentral Kansas sites this morning, but all precipitation is expected \nto remain west of the forecast area. While moisture is minimal with \nthis wave, it will bring a reinforcing shot of cold air leading to \nafternoon highs only topping out in the lower 40s today and sending \ntemperatures tumbling tonight. Overnight lows for tonight and \nWednesday night will dip into the lower 20s for most across the \nstate with highs only reaching into the middle 40s on Wednesday. \n\nBroad ridging will begin to build in over the Plains for the end of \nthe week leading to increasing thickness. We can expect temperatures \nrespond in kind with a gradual warming trend for the end of the week \nand slightly above normal temperature continuing this weekend and \ninto early next week. By Friday afternoon highs will reach into the \nmiddle to upper 50s and highs around 60 will be seen for Saturday. \nAn upper trough sweeping across the northern tier of the country \nwill just graze Kansas on Saturday. Increasing clouds and a few \nsprinkles on Saturday night for central Kansas locations will be \npossible. High temperatures in the 50s and dry conditions will then \ncontinue through the end of the forecast period with locations in \nsouthern Kansas approaching 60 many afternoons.",
